Keep the views and tracking rects the mouse-tracking walk visits alive

-[NSWindow _checkTrackingRectangles:forEvent:] snapshots a view's tracking
rects and then its subviews into C arrays with -getObjects:, which holds
no references, and calls the rect owners' mouseEntered: / mouseExited:
from inside the loop. An owner that removes a sibling view in its handler
-- a hover box it puts up on entering and takes down on leaving, replaced
when the pointer goes straight from one such view to the next -- frees
that sibling if the view was its only owner, and the walk then reaches
the freed entry and sends it -isHidden:

    objc_msgSend_fpret
    -[NSWindow _checkTrackingRectangles:forEvent:]   (x7, one per level)
    -[NSWindow sendEvent:]

An owner that removes its own tracking rect from a handler is the same
bug one loop earlier. Whether it dies depends on what the allocator has
done with the chunk since; XFormsViewer's input.xhtml died every time the
pointer moved from the second input's hint badge to the first's.

Retain the entries of both snapshots for the duration of the loop. A
removed view or rect is harmless to visit; a freed one is not.

A standalone reproduction is in nswindow-tracking-walk-freed-subview.m:
on an unpatched gnustep-gui, valgrind reports the invalid read at the
-isHidden in the subview loop, freed by -[NSView removeFromSuperview]
inside a mouseEntered:. With this patch it survives and valgrind is
clean.

diff --git a/Source/NSWindow.m b/Source/NSWindow.m
index de8ae71e2..930973524 100644
--- a/Source/NSWindow.m
+++ b/Source/NSWindow.m
@@ -3961,6 +3961,14 @@ checkCursorRectanglesExited(NSView *theView,  NSEvent *theEvent, NSPoint lastPoi
 	  lastPoint = [theView convertPoint: lastPoint fromView: nil];
 	  loc = [theView convertPoint: loc fromView: nil];
           [tr getObjects: rects];
+          /* The owners' mouseEntered: / mouseExited: run inside this loop
+           * and may remove tracking rects (their own included); the
+           * snapshot holds no references, so keep the entries alive for
+           * the loop. */
+          for (i = 0; i < count; ++i)
+            {
+              RETAIN(rects[i]);
+            }
 
           for (i = 0; i < count; ++i)
             {
@@ -4044,6 +4052,10 @@ checkCursorRectanglesExited(NSView *theView,  NSEvent *theEvent, NSPoint lastPoi
                     }
                 }
             }
+          for (i = 0; i < count; ++i)
+            {
+              RELEASE(rects[i]);
+            }
         }
     }
 
@@ -4061,6 +4073,15 @@ checkCursorRectanglesExited(NSView *theView,  NSEvent *theEvent, NSPoint lastPoi
           NSUInteger i;
 
           [sb getObjects: subs];
+          /* A mouseEntered: / mouseExited: sent while walking one subview
+           * may remove another from theView -- a hover box a badge takes
+           * down -- and theView may have been its only owner. The snapshot
+           * holds no references, so keep the subviews alive for the walk:
+           * a removed one is harmless to visit, a freed one is not. */
+          for (i = 0; i < count; ++i)
+            {
+              RETAIN(subs[i]);
+            }
           for (i = 0; i < count; ++i)
             {
               if (![subs[i] isHidden])
@@ -4068,6 +4089,10 @@ checkCursorRectanglesExited(NSView *theView,  NSEvent *theEvent, NSPoint lastPoi
                   (*ctImp)(self, ctSel, subs[i], theEvent);
                 }
             }
+          for (i = 0; i < count; ++i)
+            {
+              RELEASE(subs[i]);
+            }
         }
     }
 }
